<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-Set-Operations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Set Theory</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> The way we talk about probability is inherently rooted in set theory; events are simply subsets of a larger sample space. The problems provided here will help you become more comfortable with the fundamental concepts of set theory that are most frequently used in probability. 
    </p>


      <p> Each problem set contains two types of problems: interactive exercises and non-interactive ("written") exercises. You will be able to check your answers and get immediate feedback on the interactive portion.
      </p>

  
  </introduction>

